<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaunas</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kovno</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaunas, historically Kovno, is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neris">Neris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Nemunas</a> rivers. Grown around a 14th-century castle, it was the seat of the German Ober Ost military administration during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and served as Lithuania's temporary capital from 1920 to 1939, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> was held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Its interwar modernist architecture is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaunas.jpg" alt="Kaunas" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BigHead · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kauno_senamiestis_by_Augustas_Didzgalvis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
